# Test: the looking glass tells the operator when the engine cannot answer
# VALIDATES: AC-12 -- the engine-unavailable signal reaches a watching browser
#            over the same unnamed stream that carries the peer rows
# PREVENTS: the silent half of the htmx 4 cutover. The stream used to send this
#           as a SECOND named event, `peer-error`, which nothing swapped even
#           under htmx 2 and which htmx 4 would only dispatch as a DOM event.
#           An operator watching a frozen table would be told nothing at all.

option=server:kind=lg-no-engine
option=timeout:value=90s

action=open:path=/lg/peers

# POSITIVE CONTROL. The page rendered, and the engine really is unreachable:
# the banner is the page's own render of the same producer the stream reads
# (engineError, internal/component/lg/handler_ui.go).
expect=element:text=BGP Peers
expect=html:contains=error-banner
expect=html:contains=bgp engine unavailable

# PRE-CONDITION. The table body is empty at load, so the assertion below reads
# a state that CHANGED. `stream-error` is written by peersStreamError alone,
# and no page render calls it.
expect=html:not-contains=stream-error

# The stream ticks every 5 seconds. The row can only have arrived through a
# swapped unnamed message: nothing polls this table, and the page that produced
# the DOM had no such row in it.
action=wait:ms=8000
expect=html:contains=<tr class="stream-error">
expect=html:contains=>bgp engine unavailable</td>
